---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectTabs from '../../../components/ProjectTabs.astro';
import OursStageExplorer from '../../../components/OursStageExplorer.astro';
import StageSpectrum from '../../../components/StageSpectrum.astro';
import ExperimentStatus from '../../../components/ExperimentStatus.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../../utils/paths';
const image = (name: string) => withBase('ours/ffhq64-a1-d256/' + name);
const fig = (name: string) => withBase('decks/next-frequency-2026-10-19/figures/' + name);
const deck = withBase('projects/next-frequency/deck/');
---
<Base title="Next-frequency generation | Generation studies" description="A1 next-frequency generation: FFHQ-64 results against DCTdiff and a joint control, the UniPC sampler, stage samples, CIFAR-10 and current experiments." section="next-frequency">
  <ProjectTabs active="overview" />
  <article>
    <header class="project-hero shell">
      <div class="hero-meta"><span>Our method / A1 next-frequency</span><span>FFHQ-64 · CIFAR-10 · status 19 Oct 2026</span></div>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-intro"><h1>Next-frequency generation.</h1><p class="hero-subtitle">An image, built one frequency group at a time.</p></div>
        <div class="hero-result" aria-label="Current FFHQ-64 result">
          <p class="result-context">FFHQ-64 <span>·</span> FID-50K <span>·</span> validated UniPC samplers</p>
          <div class="hero-metric"><strong>9.46</strong><span>A1 FID-50K<br />11.1M parameters</span></div>
          <p class="hero-criterion"><span class="criterion-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>DCTdiff (44.2M), same solver: 8.68</p>
          <p class="hero-result-source">Close in quality at a quarter of the parameters, but not yet cheaper: A1 uses 46 serial network calls against DCTdiff’s 15 and took 1.72× its training H100-hours.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-deck"><p class="hero-contribution">A1 generates four groups of block-DCT coefficients in order, G0 → G3. Each group is a whole 8 × 8 field of tokens, sampled jointly by a conditional diffusion ODE inside one axial transformer.</p><div class="hero-links"><a class="text-link" href={deck}>Open the lab deck <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#samples">Follow the four stages <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="hero-figure hero-figure-contain"><a href={fig('a1-250k-unipc-grid.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open A1 FFHQ-64 sample grid"><img src={fig('a1-250k-unipc-grid.png')} alt="32 A1 FFHQ-64 samples at 250k steps with the UniPC sampler" fetchpriority="high" /></a><figcaption><span>A1 · 250k · UniPC [20, 10, 8, 8]</span><span>Seeds 0–31 of the fixed seed set, uncurated. Two show the single-token G3 checkerboard described under limits.</span></figcaption></figure>
    </header>

    <div class="shell story-grid">
      <aside class="story-nav" aria-label="On this page"><span class="nav-label">On this page</span><a href="#question">Method</a><a href="#ffhq">FFHQ-64 results</a><a href="#sampler">Sampler</a><a href="#samples">Stage samples</a><a href="#cifar">CIFAR-10</a><a href="#limits">Limits and next</a></aside>
      <div class="story-content">
        <section id="question" class="story-section question-section">
          <p class="section-overline">The research question</p><h2>Can frequency be the autoregressive axis?</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">A1 factorises an image as p(G0) · p(G1 | G0) · p(G2 | G0, G1) · p(G3 | G0…G2), autoregressive across frequency and parallel across space.</p>
          <div class="question-columns">
            <div><h3>What the model is</h3><p>The representation is DCTdiff’s: YCbCr 4:2:0, 4 × 4 block DCT, 9 zig-zag coefficients. Groups follow u + v and hold 6, 12, 18 and 18 channels per token. One 11.1M-parameter axial transformer has three spatial heads and one causal frequency head per block, with exact caching of the clean prefix.</p></div>
            <div><h3>What it is compared with</h3><p><strong>A0</strong> is the same network with every group noisy at one shared time and bidirectional frequency attention, so A1 − A0 isolates the causal factorisation. <strong>R0</strong> is our port of DCTdiff (44.2M), a joint diffusion model in the same DCT space; A1 − R0 measures practical competitiveness.</p></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="ffhq" class="story-section ours-section">
          <div class="section-heading-row"><p class="section-overline">FFHQ-64 / FID-50K / q90 reference</p><span class="evidence-tag">Completed evaluations</span></div>
          <h2>Ahead of its joint control, close to DCTdiff.</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">At equal training time A1 beats A0. Trained further and sampled with UniPC it comes within 1.09× of DCTdiff, which gets the same solver.</p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="FFHQ-64 FID-50K results" tabindex="0"><table class="comparison-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Steps · training H100-h</th><th scope="col">Sampler · calls</th><th scope="col">FID-50K ↓</th><th scope="col">Recall</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">A0 · joint axial, 11.1M</th><td data-label="Steps · training H100-h">174k · 3.34</td><td data-label="Sampler · calls">Euler · 100</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">18.49</td><td data-label="Recall">0.286</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">A1 · next-frequency, 11.1M</th><td data-label="Steps · training H100-h">146k · 3.36</td><td data-label="Sampler · calls">Euler · 400</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">15.08</td><td data-label="Recall">0.323</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">R0 · DCTdiff (our port), 44.2M</th><td data-label="Steps · training H100-h">250k · 3.34</td><td data-label="Sampler · calls">Euler · 100</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">12.66</td><td data-label="Recall">0.375</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">A1, trained further</th><td data-label="Steps · training H100-h">250k · 5.77</td><td data-label="Sampler · calls">Euler · 400</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">13.69</td><td data-label="Recall">0.347</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">R0 · DCTdiff</th><td data-label="Steps · training H100-h">250k · 3.34</td><td data-label="Sampler · calls">UniPC · 15</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">8.68</td><td data-label="Recall">0.440</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">A1, trained further</th><td data-label="Steps · training H100-h">250k · 5.77</td><td data-label="Sampler · calls">UniPC · 46</td><td data-label="FID-50K ↓">9.46</td><td data-label="Recall">0.413</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="result-footnote">The first three rows are the equal-budget comparison (3.34 H100-hours each; step counts differ because throughput differs). Per training FLOP, A1 and A0 roughly tie: A1 spends 31.1 GFLOP per training image against A0’s 17.8. The A1 Euler row at 250k is 13.75 on the paired H100 seed set used for the sampler comparison. One training seed per arm. <a href="https://forge.coreweave.com/wandb/nadavoteam/dctdiff-ffhq64/runs/wqa3lin4">A1 run ↗</a> · <a href="https://forge.coreweave.com/wandb/nadavoteam/dctdiff-ffhq64/runs/229cfxlk">A0 run ↗</a> · <a href="https://forge.coreweave.com/wandb/nadavoteam/dctdiff-ffhq64/runs/3aanusfn">R0 run ↗</a></p>
          <figure class="plot-figure"><img src={fig('frontier_abc.png')} alt="Three scatter plots of FID-50K against sampling GPU-seconds, training H100-hours and parameter count for A1, R0, A0 and BandJoint" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Where A1 stands. A: FID-50K against sampling GPU-seconds (H100, batch 500). B: against training H100-hours. C: against parameters, marker area proportional to recall. Filled markers use UniPC, hollow ones Euler. DCTdiff dominates on sampling cost and training cost; on size, neither model dominates.</figcaption></figure>
          <details class="technical-detail"><summary>Equal-step samples at 100k <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
            <div class="ours-pair"><figure><img src={image('a0-grid-100k.png')} alt="A0 joint DCT generated FFHQ-64 samples at 100k steps" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A0 · joint axial · 100k steps</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={image('a1-grid-100k.png')} alt="A1 next-frequency generated FFHQ-64 samples at 100k steps" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A1 · next-frequency · 100k steps</figcaption></figure></div>
            <p>Equal optimizer steps, Euler sampler, different draws. Monitoring FID-10K at 100k: A0 25.17, A1 20.09.</p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section id="sampler" class="story-section ours-section">
          <div class="section-heading-row"><p class="section-overline">Sampler / same frozen A1 weights</p><span class="evidence-tag">Paired noise, isolated H100 benchmark</span></div>
          <h2>400 network calls down to 46.</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">The original Euler sampler gave every group 100 calls. A UniPC schedule that gives G0 the most, [20, 10, 8, 8], improves FID-50K from 13.75 to 9.46 and recall from 0.349 to 0.413.</p>
          <figure class="plot-figure"><img src={fig('unipc_pareto.png')} alt="FID-50K against GPU-seconds and against latency for A1 and R0 samplers" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FID-50K against batch-500 GPU-seconds (a) and batch-16 latency (b). The A1 schedule is 8.0× cheaper in throughput and 9.3× in latency than Euler. DCTdiff with UniPC-15 still has lower FID and lower cost.</figcaption></figure>
          <p class="result-footnote">G0 errors amplify three to four times into later groups, which is why G0 needs 15–20 calls while G2 and G3 tolerate 8. The solver is not an architectural contribution, and fewer sampling calls do not make training cheaper.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="samples" class="story-section ours-section">
          <div class="section-heading-row"><p class="section-overline">FFHQ-64 / A1 / 146k stage sheet</p><span class="evidence-tag">Saved Euler samples</span></div>
          <h2>One face through four cumulative stages.</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">Choose a fixed sample and move from G0 to G0–G3. G0 alone fixes the layout: re-sampling only its noise accounts for 98% of pixel variation.</p>
          <OursStageExplorer />
          <StageSpectrum />
          <a class="text-link" href={withBase('projects/generation-step-by-step/#instruments')}>Compare this trajectory with DCTdiff and FAR in the streaming report <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </section>

        <section id="cifar" class="story-section ours-section">
          <div class="section-heading-row"><p class="section-overline">CIFAR-10 / FID-10K / Euler</p><span class="evidence-tag">Completed, earlier study</span></div>
          <h2>Close at 100k steps, behind at maturity.</h2>
          <p class="section-lead">At 100k steps A1 cost about 5% FID against A0 and met the pre-registered STRONG criterion. With a wider network trained to 250k, A0 kept improving and A1 levelled off.</p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="CIFAR-10 results" tabindex="0"><table class="comparison-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">A1 next-frequency</th><th scope="col">A0 joint axial</th><th scope="col">Reading</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">D192 · 100k</th><td data-label="A1 next-frequency">18.43</td><td data-label="A0 joint axial">≈17.5</td><td data-label="Reading">A1 / A0 = 1.05, STRONG</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">D256 · 250k</th><td data-label="A1 next-frequency">13.16</td><td data-label="A0 joint axial">10.97</td><td data-label="Reading">A0 / A1 = 0.83; DCTdiff 8.50</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <figure class="plot-figure plot-figure-narrow"><img src={fig('cifar_fid_vs_h100.png')} alt="CIFAR-10 FID-10K against training H100-hours for R0, A0 and A1" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>CIFAR-10 FID-10K against training H100-hours. The causal penalty grows with training; FFHQ-64 reversed this ranking. CIFAR and FFHQ FIDs are on different scales.</figcaption></figure>
        </section>

        <section id="limits" class="story-section status-section">
          <p class="section-overline">Limits and next experiments</p><h2>What is established, and what is open.</h2>
          <div class="status-rows">
            <div><ExperimentStatus status="completed" /><p>FFHQ-64: A1 beats its joint control at equal steps and hours, and reaches FID-50K 9.46 against DCTdiff’s 8.68. It is not yet cheaper to train or sample.</p></div>
            <div><ExperimentStatus status="completed" text="Completed screen" /><p>Reversing the order (G3 → G0) collapses quality at 100k: FID-10K 46.8 against 13.7. The pre-registered per-stage loss write-up is pending.</p></div>
            <div><ExperimentStatus status="completed" text="Completed diagnosis" /><p>Later groups are under-dispersed even with a real prefix (spread / skill 0.83 and 0.81 for G2 and G3), and the gap compounds along the chain.</p></div>
            <div><ExperimentStatus status="ongoing" /><p>Per-group variance preconditioning: EMA FID-10K −12% at 50k, raw-weight FID +0.6%, recall +0.07 to +0.08; borderline and not yet adjudicated. A depthwise 3 × 3 local-mixer pilot is training.</p></div>
            <div><ExperimentStatus status="proposed" /><p>A diffusion-free conditional flow head (gates passed, pilot queued) and coefficient-level ordering with nine stages (authorized, not run).</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="tail-strip">
            <div class="tail-strip-images">{[0, 1, 2, 3].map((k) => <figure><img src={fig(`tail-failure-g${k}.png`)} alt={`Failing sample decoded through G${k}`} width="128" height="128" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>{k === 0 ? 'G0' : `≤ G${k}`}</figcaption></figure>)}</div>
            <p>Robustness: in about 2.6% of UniPC samples one token’s G3 coefficients explode into a single-token checkerboard when that token’s own noise is beyond about 3σ. Shown is the worst of 5,000 samples. It costs nothing measurable in FID and also occurs in the joint control.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="end-note"><span>Source boundary</span><p>Numbers come from the campaign’s result files; the <a href={deck}>lab deck</a> lists the source of each figure and its appendix gives protocols, samplers and the full screen table. The 146k images and their hashes are in <a href={image('source.json')}>the source manifest</a>.</p><a class="text-link" href={deck}>Open the lab deck <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
